# Citation metadata for nn_l1_sbi (CFF 1.2.0).
#
# GitHub renders this file ("Cite this repository") and Zenodo reads it when a release is
# archived, so it must stay valid YAML *and* valid CFF.
#
# Deliberately incomplete: the accompanying paper has no journal, year, DOI or arXiv id yet,
# and this repository has no Zenodo DOI yet. Those fields are LEFT OUT rather than filled
# with placeholders, because CFF types them (`year` must be an integer, `doi` must match a
# real DOI pattern) and a placeholder there would make the file invalid — and a plausible
# fake identifier is worse than a missing one. The TODOs below say exactly what to add.
#
# TODO(before release), in this file:
#   * top level:          `doi:` / `identifiers:` — the Zenodo DOI of the archived release
#   * top level:          `date-released:` — the release date, as YYYY-MM-DD
#   * preferred-citation: `journal:` — the journal, on acceptance
#   * preferred-citation: `year:` and `volume:` / `pages:` — on publication
#   * preferred-citation: `doi:` and/or `identifiers:` with the arXiv id — once posted
# Keep this in sync with the BibTeX block in README.md ("Citation"), which carries the same
# TODOs, and with .zenodo.json.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite both the software itself (this file) and the paper
  it accompanies (see `preferred-citation`).
title: nn_l1_sbi
abstract: >-
  Companion code for a weak-lensing simulation-based-inference study that compares
  wavelet (starlet) l1-norm summary statistics against learned convolutional compressors
  on tomographic convergence maps.
type: software
version: 1.0.0
license: MIT
repository-code: https://github.com/AndreasTersenov/nn_l1_sbi

authors:
  # No ORCID is recorded here: none is known to this repository, and CFF gives no way to
  # write an unknown one. Add `orcid: https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000` when available.
  - given-names: Andreas
    family-names: Tersenov
    email: andrewtersenov@gmail.com

keywords:
  - weak gravitational lensing
  - simulation-based inference
  - summary statistics
  - wavelets
  - neural compression
  - cosmology

preferred-citation:
  type: article
  title: >-
    Do we need deep learning for tomographic weak lensing? Wavelets versus neural
    compression
  authors:
    - given-names: Andreas
      family-names: Tersenov
    - given-names: Jean-Luc
      family-names: Starck
    - given-names: Martin
      family-names: Kilbinger
  notes: >-
    TODO — the journal, year, volume, pages, DOI and arXiv identifier of this paper are not
    assigned yet and are deliberately absent from this record rather than guessed. Fill them
    in here, in the BibTeX block of README.md, and in .zenodo.json once they exist.
